    "notes": [
      {
        "category": "summary",
        "text": "Security update for python-pypdf",
        "title": "Title of the patch"
      },
      {
        "category": "description",
        "text": "This update for python-pypdf fixes the following issues:\n\nChanges in python-pypdf:\n\n- CVE-2026-113786: PDF containing image data labeled with the\n  `/DCTDecode` filter causes Pillow to select and invoke an\n  unintended image parser when the image is loaded (bsc#1286632)\n- CVE-2026-113789: crafted PDF causes large memory usage or long\n  runtimes when an application retrieves the document's form fields\n  (bsc#1286633)\n- CVE-2026-113790: PDF containing arrays or dictionaries with many\n  elements causes parsing to consume large amounts of memory\n  (bsc#1286634)\n- CVE-2026-113791: PDF containing a content stream with many small\n  operations causes parsing to consume large amounts of memory\n  (bsc#1286635)\n- CVE-2026-113792: calling `PdfWriter.find_outline_item` on a\n  crafted PDF with a cyclic outline hierarchy can enter an infinite\n  loop and block the current process (bsc#1286636)\n- CVE-2026-113793: removing an object from a crafted cyclic PDF\n  tree can enter an infinite loop and block the current process\n  (bsc#1286637)\n- CVE-2026-113794: incomplete PDF that ends inside a standard\n  cross-reference table causes pypdf to mishandle the unexpected\n  end of file and enter an infinite loop (bsc#1286638)\n- CVE-2026-113795: accessing a crafted PDF stream with a large\n  number of filters or multiple filters that pass around large\n  amounts of data can cause large memory consumption (bsc#1286639)\n",
        "title": "Description of the patch"
      },
      {
